;;; A query's `:goal` splits a name that starts with `neq` —
;;; regression `kw-neq-in-goal` (`corpus/regressions.toml`): a reserved word is a
;;; keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(neqw a)`; a hypothesis rule, `h`, proposing `(tag ?x)` for
;;; each `(p ?x)`; and a query, `(neqw ?y)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `01-grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `neqw` is a name like any other. R is ∅ and
;;; {(tag a)}, and the query asks about `neqw`, which no hypothesis rule proposes: the
;;; read-out names it `outside` the universe, and the query's one class is `?y
;;; = a`. It is the answer of `kw-neq-in-goal-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the goal `(neqw ?y)` as the
;;; guard `(neq w ?y)`. R was right, but the query printed as `(neq w ?y)`, *the
;;; goal holds: it has no variable*, and the read-out named nothing `outside`
;;; the universe. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode
;;; and `ein oracle` the same.) A keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary
;;; `01-grammar.md` § 1 states (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?3}") (relation neqw Node :why "neqw holds of {?3}") (relation tag Node :why "{?3} is tagged")
(p a) (neqw a)
(rule h () :match (p ?x) :assert (open (tag ?x)) :why "p holds of {?x}; guess that {?x} is tagged")
(query :goal (neqw ?y))
